afk: plans must expose parallelism; implementers do not commit.

Motivation: execution happens as parallel waves of isolated subagents
(see skills-subagent-driven-development.patch), so every plan needs a
machine-followable map of which tasks can run concurrently, and
per-task git commit steps are meaningless — the orchestrator lands each
task by cherry-picking its parked ref.

What must survive a rebase:
- Plan header REQUIRES a "## Dependency Map" section: per-task
  depends-on edges derived from the Interfaces Consumes/Produces
  blocks, plus wave grouping; guidance to minimize edges (wide waves
  beat deep chains).
- Task template carries "**Depends on:**" and ends at the passing-test
  step — no commit step, no "frequent commits" anywhere.
- Self-review includes the dependency check (map matches Interfaces,
  acyclic, no edge without a consumed interface).
- Execution handoff describes option 1 as parallel isolated subagents
  per Dependency Map wave.
- Dropped on purpose: the worktree provisioning context line.

